## 4) Kubernetes manifests (k8s/overlays/dev/*.yaml)

Below is a single example (auth). The other two services mirror this with image & port changes.

`k8s/overlays/dev/auth-deployment.yaml`

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: microservices-dev
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: auth-service
  namespace: microservices-dev
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: auth-service
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: auth-service
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: auth-service
        image: <REGISTRY>/auth-service:IMAGE_TAG
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /auth/health
            port: 8080
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 5
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /auth/health
            port: 8080
          initialDelaySeconds: 15
          periodSeconds: 10
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: auth-service
  namespace: microservices-dev
spec:
  selector:
    app: auth-service
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
```

Create similar `user-deployment.yaml` and `payment-deployment.yaml` replacing names, image names, and ports.

An optional `ingress.yaml` can route `/auth`, `/user`, `/payment` paths to services.

